<TopicPage slug="subjects/economics/advanced/development-economics">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an advanced Economics lesson. It assumes the two foundational lessons —
    <em>Supply and Demand</em> and <em>Money and Inflation</em> — and the intermediate rungs
    that matter most here: <em>GDP &amp; National Accounts</em> (what GDP measures and what
    it omits), <em>Market Failure &amp; Externalities</em>, <em>Elasticity</em> and
    <em>Unemployment &amp; Labour Markets</em>. Development economics takes those tools to
    the discipline's highest-stakes questions: why some countries stay poor, and what
    actually lifts them.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain why GDP per head needs PPP correction, and what the Human Development Index adds and still misses;</li>
    <li>define absolute and relative poverty, and read the extreme-poverty line as a PPP floor;</li>
    <li>model a poverty trap as an S-shaped savings curve and state what a "big push" claims;</li>
    <li>analyse the demographic transition, the dependency ratio and the one-time demographic dividend;</li>
    <li>weigh geography, culture and institutions as rival explanations for why countries differ.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Development economics asks the discipline's highest-stakes question: <strong>why are some
    countries poor, and what actually lifts them?</strong> It is where economics meets its
    conscience — and where, for most of the twentieth century, confident ideology ran ahead of
    evidence. The modern field inverted that: measure living standards honestly, define poverty
    precisely, test interventions like medicines, and treat grand theories with suspicion until
    the data speak. The results have been part triumphant (a billion people out of extreme
    poverty since 1990, overwhelmingly in Asia) and part humbling (the roughly 700 million the
    tide has not yet reached, most of them now in fragile states). This unit works through the
    four questions that organise the field: how to measure a life, how to define and break
    poverty, how population shapes the whole calculation, and why the gaps between countries
    opened in the first place.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the record: what convergence achieved, what was learned, what remains" widget="equationStep" config={devCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the four steps are the field's whole arc, in order —
    the late start of mass convergence, the poverty line's careful redefinition, the shift
    from ideology to randomised evidence, and what remains unresolved. Read step 2 twice:
    the same person counts as poor or not depending on whether money is converted at market
    exchange rates or by what it actually buys locally — the PPP correction is not cosmetic.
  </p>

  <h2>Living standards: measuring what a life is worth</h2>
  <p>
    The first problem is that the obvious ruler — GDP per capita — is a bad one in developing
    economies, where much output never passes through a market: the farm's own harvest, the
    family member caring for children, the informal stall. Comparisons must be made at
    <strong>purchasing power parity</strong> (PPP), which asks what the same money actually buys
    locally: a market exchange rate converts incomes as if a haircut cost the same in Nairobi as
    in New York, and can understate real living standards by a factor of two or three in poor
    countries — the difference between "GDP per capita $1,000" and "a person who can eat, house
    and school a family modestly". Even corrected, income is not welfare, so the
    <strong>Human Development Index</strong> combines three dimensions — life expectancy at
    birth, schooling (expected and mean years), and income — and deliberately trades off between
    them: Sri Lanka and Cuba reach lifespans near 77–78 years on modest incomes, while some
    Gulf states buy high incomes with worse social indicators. Behind the indices sit
    distribution (income growth captured by a narrow elite does little for the median
    household), gender gaps, and the capabilities approach — Amartya Sen's insistence that
    development means expanding what people can <em>do and be</em>, not what they own. Sen's
    Bengal famine analysis (1981) remains the sharpest demonstration: food was being
    <em>exported</em> from Bengal in 1943 while three million died — famine is a failure of
    entitlements and access, not necessarily of supply.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "GDP per head, PPP-adjusted, is close enough to living
    standards that the alternatives are decoration." The corrections are not cosmetic; they
    change policy. The average hides distribution — a country can post strong GDP growth for a
    decade while the median household stagnates. It ignores unpaid work, which is mostly
    women's, worth tens of per cent of measured output. It prices leisure at zero, pollution at
    zero and a felled forest as income. And it can rise while capabilities shrink — Sen's point
    is that a person with the same income but no clinic, no school and no vote is not equally
    developed. Every index the field has built since, from the HDI to the multidimensional
    poverty measures, exists to repair a specific, named defect of the income average.
  </p>

  <p><strong>So far:</strong> income averages mislead in poor economies — non-market output, PPP correction, distribution and capabilities each repair a named defect of the single number. The next question is the floor beneath the average: where the line is drawn, and whether people can escape it.</p>

  <h2>Poverty: lines, traps and exits</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Absolute poverty</strong> is an income floor measured in PPP dollars — the World
    Bank's extreme-poverty line, updated in 2022 from $1.90 to $2.15 per day (2017 prices) to
    match the national lines of the poorest countries — against <strong>relative poverty</strong>,
    typically defined in rich countries as living below 50 or 60 per cent of median income,
    which measures exclusion rather than survival. The headline numbers: about 700 million
    people — roughly 8–9 per cent of humanity — live below the extreme line today, down from
    around 38 per cent in 1990; the entire decline is concentrated in East and South Asia,
    while Sub-Saharan Africa's share of the world's extreme poor has risen to about 60 per cent.
    The analytical heart of the field is the <strong>poverty trap</strong>: a feedback loop
    where poverty causes the conditions that reproduce poverty. Make it concrete: a farmer earns
    40 a season, needs 55 worth of seed, fertiliser and storage to escape subsistence, and can
    never save the 15 gap — malaria saps her workdays, her children leave school for labour,
    and next season she earns 40 again. The S-shaped savings curve means small transfers
    evaporate but one push past the threshold changes the trajectory. Evidence for traps is
    genuinely mixed — which is itself a major finding: Kremer, Miguel and co-authors' long-run
    deworming work showed durable gains from a 40-cent-per-child drug; conversely, big
    unconditional transfers rarely generate escape velocity without complementary basics. This
    is why the RCT revolution (Nobel 2019) mattered: it converted "does aid work?" — a question
    with no meaningful answer — into "does intervention X at cost Y move outcome Z?" — dozens of
    which now have calibrated answers.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "poor countries are poor because they lack natural
    resources." Resource-poor Japan, Switzerland, Singapore and South Korea sit at the top of
    the income table while resource-rich Nigeria, Venezuela and the Democratic Republic of
    Congo underperform their geology — the <em>resource curse</em>: commodity windfalls corrupt
    institutions, appreciate the currency (killing other exports, the Dutch disease), and
    finance the very conflicts that destroy growth. What countries lack is usually not
    resources but the institutions that turn any endowment — soil, oil or people — into
    broad-based productivity.
  </p>

  <p><strong>So far:</strong> the poverty line is a measured floor, traps are feedback loops that hold people beneath it, and randomised trials converted "does aid work?" — a question with no meaningful answer — into dozens with calibrated ones. Whether a growing population helps or hurts, it turns out, is a question about timing.</p>

  <h2>Population: the dividend and the burden</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>demographic transition</strong> structures everything: societies move from high
    birth and death rates (population flat, life brutal) through a death-rate decline while
    births stay high (explosive growth) into low birth and death rates (ageing). Development
    economics cares because the transition opens a rare window: when fertility falls while the
    cohorts born earlier are still young, the <strong>dependency ratio</strong> — children plus
    elderly as a share of working age — collapses, freeing savings and female labour. East Asia
    rode exactly this "demographic dividend": South Korea's fertility fell from above 6 in 1960
    to below replacement within a generation while its working-age share swelled, and a large
    share of its growth is attributable to that arithmetic. The contrast across countries today
    is the sharpest the science has ever seen: Niger's total fertility rate is around 6.7,
    South Korea's around 0.7 — the lowest ever recorded — and China's population began falling
    in 2022 after decades of the one-child policy, with its workforce shrinking and its
    old-age share climbing toward Japan's (nearly 30 per cent of Japanese are 65 or over).
    Malthus's fear — population outrunning food — has so far been defeated by the Green
    Revolution and falling fertility, but the frontier question has inverted: for much of the
    world the development problem is no longer too many children but too few workers per
    pensioner, and whether countries can grow rich <em>before</em> they grow old. The fiscal
    arithmetic is unforgiving: Japan counted roughly ten potential workers for every person
    over 65 around 1970 and has around two today, which converts directly into pension,
    health-care and immigration pressure. The dividend, in other words, is a one-time window —
    miss it, and ageing arrives before affluence does.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "a fast-growing population makes a country poorer." More
    people mean more mouths and more workers; the effect on income <em>per head</em> depends
    entirely on timing and policy. A young, uneducated, land-scarce population strains budgets;
    the same population, educated and employed at the transition's sweet spot, is the dividend
    that built East Asia. Bangladesh's fertility fell from about 7 to about 2 largely through
    education and family-planning access — and its growth accelerated, not despite that, but
    partly because of it.
  </p>

  <p><strong>So far:</strong> the demographic transition opens a one-time dividend window — fertility falling while earlier cohorts are still of working age — and missing it means ageing before affluence. The deepest question remains: why did the windows open for some countries and not others?</p>

  <h2>Why countries differ: geography, institutions and luck</h2>
  <p>
    The deepest question in the field is dispersion: why did Ghana and South Korea — with
    roughly comparable incomes per head around 1960, in the low four figures in today's money —
    end up with an income gap of roughly eight-fold? The candidate explanations form a
    centuries-long argument. <strong>Geography</strong>: disease ecology (malaria made tropical
    interiors lethal to settlement and livestock), landlocked access, climate; Sachs's version
    holds these traps are real and need direct attack. <strong>Culture</strong>: trust, work
    norms, colonial languages. <strong>Institutions</strong>: the most influential modern
    hypothesis (Acemoglu, Johnson and Robinson, Nobel 2024) runs through colonial settler
    mortality — where Europeans could settle, they built inclusive property rights and courts;
    where disease killed them, they built <em>extractive</em> institutions designed to ship
    wealth out, and the extractive institutions outlived the empires. Korea versus Korea is the
    field's cleanest experiment: one people, one culture, one geography, split in 1945 — and a
    nighttime satellite photograph now marks the institutional border. The honest synthesis is
    that none of the three is sufficient alone: geography shapes what institutions were built,
    institutions decide whether a policy can work, and policy decides the decade. What the
    evidence has killed is fatalism: Botswana (diamonds, good institutions, decades of
    Africa's fastest growth) and Vietnam (Doi Moi reforms from 1986, poverty falling from
    around 60 per cent to below 5) show trajectories bend when the rules change.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Why is GDP per capita converted at purchasing power parity when comparing living standards across countries — and what can it still miss even after that correction?</li>
    <li>A farmer earns 40 a season but needs 55 worth of inputs to escape subsistence. Explain precisely why this is a trap, and what a "big push" claims will change at the threshold.</li>
    <li>South Korea and Ghana had roughly comparable incomes around 1960; the gap is now roughly eight-fold. Which explanation does the Korea-versus-Korea comparison support, and why is it such a clean test?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Because market exchange rates convert money as if prices
    were identical everywhere, understating real living standards in poor countries by a
    factor of two or three; PPP asks what the same money actually buys locally. Even
    corrected, GDP per head misses distribution, unpaid work, leisure, pollution and Sen's
    capabilities — what people can do and be, not just what they own. (2) The farmer can
    never save the 15-unit gap, and poverty reproduces itself — illness cuts workdays,
    children leave school for labour — so next season she earns 40 again. On the S-shaped
    curve, small transfers evaporate while one push past the threshold changes the
    trajectory; that is the claim the randomised evidence tests. (3) Institutions. One
    people, one culture, one geography, split in 1945 — every rival explanation is held
    constant, so the diverging rules each half built are what remains; the nighttime
    satellite photograph marks the border of the hypothesis.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>GDP per head must be PPP-corrected, and even then misses distribution, unpaid work and capabilities.</li>
    <li>Extreme poverty is a measured floor — about USD 2.15 per day at PPP — not a vague condition.</li>
    <li>Poverty traps are feedback loops; on an S-shaped curve, one push past the threshold changes the trajectory.</li>
    <li>RCTs converted "does aid work?" into "does intervention X at cost Y move outcome Z?"</li>
    <li>The demographic dividend is a one-time window: grow rich before growing old.</li>
    <li>Institutions outperform geography and culture in the cleanest tests — fatalism is what the evidence killed.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The sibling advanced lessons surround this one: <em>International Trade &amp;
    Comparative Advantage</em> is the channel through which the convergence in the widget's
    first step actually happened, <em>Game Theory</em> formalises the bargaining over terms
    of trade and the dilemmas between nations, and <em>Behavioural Economics</em> questions
    whether households under scarcity optimise the way the standard models assume.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The RCT wars.</strong> The randomised evaluation movement made causal claims clean
    at the scale of the trial — and drew two serious critiques. First, external validity: a
    bednet trial in one district need not travel; second, Angus Deaton's (Nobel 2015) deeper
    objection that the questions that matter most — institutions, growth, structural change —
    cannot be randomised at all. The mature position treats RCTs as one instrument in the
    orchestra, best for micro-interventions, and leans on natural experiments (the Korean
    split, colonial history, rainfall shocks) for the big questions.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The aid argument, sharpened.</strong> Sachs's <em>End of Poverty</em> argued a big
    push can end the trap; Easterly's <em>White Man's Burden</em> replied that planners fail
    where searchers rule and that aid can prop up bad governments. Forty years of data support
    a careful middle: project-level aid with good evaluation works often; system-level,
    geopolitically-driven aid has a poor record; and the strongest single correlate of
    take-off remains domestic institutional and policy quality — which outside money buys far
    less than its donors hope.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The frontier.</strong> The last mile is now concentrated where measurement is
    hardest: conflict zones, weak states, and countries where the poverty line is a rounding
    error of the GDP statistics. Growth economists debate the <em>middle-income trap</em> — why
    so few countries graduate from middle to high income (South Korea, Taiwan and Singapore
    did; Brazil, Mexico and Thailand have not). Technology is the wildcard: M-Pesa's mobile
    money reached the unbanked faster than any branch could, and remote-sensing data now
    estimates village-level poverty from night lights. And the climate century entangles
    everything: the countries least responsible for emissions face the largest adaptation
    bills, which turns development economics and climate finance into one subject.
  </p>

